// Every markup rule in `std/vyx-hints`, over every `.vyx` file the site has.
//
// A SEPARATE PROGRAM, and not an import in `site/export.vyrn`. It used to be
// FORCED: `std/vyx-hints` reaches `std/hints`, which declared `Policy`, and
// `std/http` declares `Policy` too — a top-level name is program-wide, so the
// two could not be linked and the export links `std/http`. That is fixed;
// `std/hints` calls its record `HintPolicy` now, and the two modules link.
//
// It stays separate because that is the better shape, not because it must. The
// rules scan sixty-one `.vyx` files and the export does not need them to
// publish; running them here keeps the two failures apart, so a broken template
// says so instead of failing a build step that is about something else.
//
// The rules are errors. `vyrn check site/markup.vyrn` fails the build when a
// template breaks one, and that is the whole point: `std/vyx-hints` had
// nineteen rules and one caller, its own tests. A rule nothing runs is a rule
// the next page can break.
import { vyxHints } from "std/vyx-hints"
import * as siteMarkup from vyxHints("./app")

fn main() -> Int64 {
    // Read so the import cannot be dropped as unused. The number is every `.vyx`
    // file that was parsed and checked; a file that stops being discovered
    // shows up here as a smaller number.
    print(siteMarkup.vyxHintsChecked())
    return 0
}
